# Introduction

Laryngeal cancer represents a significant clinical challenge with considerable morbidity and mortality burden [PMID:35910213; PMID:40229748]. The identification of prognostic biomarkers is essential for understanding patient survival outcomes and informing clinical management strategies. Transcriptomic profiling approaches have been applied to identify prognostic signatures in laryngeal cancer, with studies demonstrating the utility of differentially expressed genes in prognostic assessment [PMID:35910213; PMID:40229748; PMID:39085122]. These investigations have employed Cox regression analysis and survival analysis methods to evaluate the prognostic significance of gene expression patterns, establishing the foundation for biomarker discovery in this disease context.

Systematic screening approaches that integrate differential expression analysis with survival analysis continue to provide valuable insights for prognostic biomarker identification. Previous studies have demonstrated that differentially expressed genes can serve as a candidate pool for prognostic screening, with univariate Cox regression analysis enabling the identification of genes associated with patient survival outcomes [PMID:35910213; PMID:40229748]. These approaches have revealed potential prognostic markers in laryngeal cancer, supporting the continued application of transcriptomic screening methods for biomarker discovery.

The present study aimed to identify prognostic biomarkers in laryngeal cancer through a systematic transcriptomic screening approach. We performed differential expression analysis using the edgeR package to establish a candidate gene pool, followed by univariate Cox regression analysis to evaluate the prognostic significance of the differentially expressed genes. This approach enables the screening and identification of genes associated with patient survival outcomes, providing candidate biomarkers that warrant further investigation.

